.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/d3-array Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "d3-array"
.PP
Data in JavaScript is often represented by an iterable (such as an \fRarray\fP, \fRset\fP or \fRgenerator\fP), and so iterable manipulation is a common task when analyzing or visualizing data\&. For example, you might take a contiguous slice (subset) of an array, filter an array using a predicate function, or map an array to a parallel set of values using a transform function\&. Before looking at the methods that d3-array provides, familiarize yourself with the powerful \fRarray methods built-in to JavaScript\fP\&.

.PP
JavaScript includes \fBmutation methods\fP that modify the array:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.pop\fP - Remove the last element from the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.push\fP - Add one or more elements to the end of the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.reverse\fP - Reverse the order of the elements of the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.shift\fP - Remove the first element from the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.sort\fP - Sort the elements of the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.splice\fP - Add or remove elements from the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.unshift\fP - Add one or more elements to the front of the array\&.
.PP

.PP
There are also \fBaccess methods\fP that return some representation of the array:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.concat\fP - Join the array with other array(s) or value(s)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.join\fP - Join all elements of the array into a string\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.slice\fP - Extract a section of the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.indexOf\fP - Find the first occurrence of a value within the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.lastIndexOf\fP - Find the last occurrence of a value within the array\&.
.PP

.PP
And finally \fBiteration methods\fP that apply functions to elements in the array:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.filter\fP - Create a new array with only the elements for which a predicate is true\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.forEach\fP - Call a function for each element in the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.every\fP - See if every element in the array satisfies a predicate\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.map\fP - Create a new array with the result of calling a function on every element in the array\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.some\fP - See if at least one element in the array satisfies a predicate\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.reduce\fP - Apply a function to reduce the array to a single value (from left-to-right)\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fIarray\fP\&.reduceRight\fP - Apply a function to reduce the array to a single value (from right-to-left)\&.
.PP
.SH "Installing"
.PP
If you use npm, \fRnpm install d3-array\fP\&. You can also download the \fRlatest release on GitHub\fP\&. For vanilla HTML in modern browsers, import d3-array from jsDelivr:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script type="module">

import {min} from "https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-array@3/+esm";

const m = min(array);

</script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
For legacy environments, you can load d3-array’s UMD bundle; a \fRd3\fP global is exported:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script src="https://cdn\&.jsdelivr\&.net/npm/d3\-array@3"></script>
<script>

const m = d3\&.min(array);

</script>
.fi
.PP
